{
  "about": "The column-name reader's contract, as data. Every implementation of the reader - this package's nameReader.ts and any server-side twin - must tokenise these names, fold these strings and find these tokens exactly as written here. Hand-written, not generated from either implementation: a fixture produced by the code it checks proves nothing.",
  "rules": {
    "words": "NFC; camelCase split where a lower-case letter or a decimal digit is followed by an upper-case letter, in any cased script; then whitespace, the ASCII separators _ - . / and every NON-ASCII character that is not a letter, mark or number separate words; other ASCII punctuation stays attached to its word; lower-cased with the locale-independent mapping.",
    "folded": "The name is folded (see fold) BEFORE it is read, so the folded words line up one-to-one with the words.",
    "fold": "One UTF-16 unit in, one out. l-stroke, dotless i, o-stroke, d-stroke and eth (both cases) fold to l, i, o, d, d; Arabic-Indic, extended Arabic-Indic, Devanagari, Thai and full-width digits fold to 0-9; full-width Latin letters fold to ASCII; any other character folds to its NFD base when every mark removed lies in U+0300-U+036F and one character remains; everything else is unchanged.",
    "views": "word: the folded token equals a folded word (unfolded for a fold-sensitive language, vi). suffix: for a compounding language (de nl sv da nb fi hu), a folded word ends with the token and keeps at least 3 code points before it. prefix: for ar and he, a word minus one of the language's glued prefixes equals the token and keeps at least 2 code points. substring: a token in Han, Hiragana, Katakana or Thai of at least 2 code points occurs inside one of the name's runs of those scripts. null: no view finds it."
  },
